-\chapter{PHM Case Study: Target Construction, Proxy Validity, and Sensor-Group Fusion} \label{ch:phm} PHM establishes the target-validity problem that precedes the paired vision--sensor studies: machine measurements predict a proxy constructed from post-run tooth photographs. The principal held-out-experiment comparison did not establish lower error for three-group sensor fusion than for a training-mean constant. Photographs supply supervision rather than an inference-time image branch in that comparison. Separate visual diagnostics examine target emulation and appearance agreement without an independent physical-damage reference.
-\section{Study Objective and Relationship to the Research Questions} The study addresses R1 by separating target validity from the correspondence and availability of the prediction inputs. For SQ1, the image and sensor experiments assessed whether frozen visual features and temporal sensor representations could reproduce a specified target. For SQ2, the principal fusion comparison combined context, root-mean-square (RMS) vibration and condition indicators, all available at inference. It did not test joint image--sensor inference. For SQ3, a predictor that already returns nearly constant values can appear insensitive to corruption, so its clean-input error matters. For SQ4, the training-mean comparison asks whether measured sensor inputs improved prediction over a rule that ignores them at inference. It cannot establish whether the photographic target measures damage. That question requires a physical reference independent of the rule.\ev{PHM target and deployment inputs}{\EvidenceRef{E021}{}}
-\section{Dataset, Acquisition Structure, and Supervision Challenges} The retained population contains EXP-A runs 1--5, EXP-B runs 1--7 and EXP-F runs 1--8: twenty nominal run units across three experiments. A run can contain multiple tooth photographs, camera-sequence views, high-frequency vibration members, low-frequency context measurements and condition-indicator records. An image, a minute-level feature row and a run are consequently different observational units. Repeated views and long sensor sequences increase the number of stored observations without increasing the number of independently acquired experiments to the same extent.
