 \section{Investigation A: Image-Derived Target Construction and Assessment} The visual investigation has three distinct layers: producing a deterministic target, fitting a model to that target, and assessing how closely that target represents physical damage. The first two layers can be inspected from code, saved targets and prediction artifacts. The third requires external reference evidence. Agreement with a heuristic mask is insufficient because it evaluates reproduction of the same rule that supplied supervision. This distinction also applies to the detector trained on heuristic pseudo-boxes: its localization metrics are defined relative to those boxes, not expert-annotated defects. The separate scalar, detector and multitask routes therefore remain different experiments with different outputs and comparison units.\ev{Visual prediction experiments}{\EvidenceRef{E025}{}}
 \subsection{Motivation and Target Definition} Without organizer-supplied continuous damage labels, an explicit and reproducible candidate-area statistic offered an engineering starting point. The implementation measures dark, horizontally extended regions within a chosen flank ROI. Its percentage denominator is the ROI area, so a change in ROI changes the numerical scale even if the same pixels are selected. The term ``candidate'' is substantive: reflectance, machining texture and acquisition artifacts may satisfy the image rule without being physical damage. The target can consequently support a study of proxy prediction and measurement sensitivity. It cannot by itself support a claim about tooth material loss or maintenance necessity.\ev{Candidate measurement function}{\EvidenceRef{E035}{measure\_damage\_candidate}}
 \subsection{Image Processing and Aggregation} The inspected target code converts RGB to Lab luminance, applies CLAHE, estimates a smooth background, and obtains a dark residual. Robust standardized residuals define candidate pixels; morphological opening and closing and component-size/aspect criteria retain extended regions. Candidate pixels divided by ROI pixels give the per-view percentage. Historical v2 view maxima and the v3 deterministic-view rule then produce tooth values, followed by the top-three run mean.
 The PHM scalar baseline invoked the Ultralytics RT-DETR-L prediction path and recorded intermediate maps from three layers. The regression head used 768 spatially pooled image features and one additional value, extraction latency. Latency depends on the computing environment and can confound interpretation of the image-regression result. Separate PHM detection and multitask experiments used heuristic pseudo-box supervision. The core REASSEMBLE classifier used the ResNet18-vd CNN backbone inside its RT-DETR-R18vd checkpoint (Chapter~6).\ev{Extraction implementation}{\EvidenceRef{E033}{extract\_features}}\ev{Image features and latency covariate}{\EvidenceRef{E024}{}}
