-\section{Synthesis, Limitations, and Motivation for Native-Outcome Paired Studies} Neither the single sensor encoder nor the three-group model improved on the training mean in the observed run-level comparison. The paired interval for three-group fusion included both lower and higher error. Changing the photographic target also changed the constant predictor's error on a fixed split. The comparison therefore challenges the inference that adding sensor representations improves prediction of this proxy across experiments.
-Optimizing a predictor for physical damage would first require checking the proxy against independent damage measurements or expert area annotations. Until then, the error estimates quantify agreement with the photographic rule. The study also leaves advance warning, remaining useful life and response to natural sensor failure unresolved. Differences in targets, prediction units and preprocessing prevent a direct comparison across all PHM fusion models.\ev{Photographic target versions}{\EvidenceRef{E023}{}}\ev{PHM experiment configurations}{\EvidenceRef{E025}{}}
