+\section{Implications for Subsequent Dataset and Architecture Choices} PHM was undertaken within the architecture programme motivated by robotic welding, rather than as a prespecified feasibility stage designed around its eventual negative result. The subsequent move to REASSEMBLE responded to supervision and comparison limits: recorded action outcomes, visual and sensor inputs at a common completed-action horizon, and more recording groups. The empirical task changed from a photograph-derived gear-condition proxy to retrospective manipulation failure, retaining the questions about representation, fusion and robustness. This progression does not validate a welding classifier or establish that visual and vibration information are generally uninformative.
 The design lesson is to establish the target, observation horizon and independent evaluation units before optimizing fusion complexity. For PHM, a physical damage measurement would establish what the proxy represents. The error of the constant predictor provides a reference for interpreting model performance under degradation. The subsequent study compares combined pipelines with their single-input reference models and measures their responses to controlled corruption.\ev{Cross-study rationale}{\EvidenceRef{E106}{}}
